# The two processes `just observatory-dev` runs, in one supervised tree.
#
# hivemind reads this file and owns both processes: when either one exits, the other is
# interrupted with it, and a signal sent to hivemind tears down both process groups. That
# is the whole reason this file exists. The recipe it replaces backgrounded the API and
# then `exec`'d Vite, which discarded the `EXIT` trap that should have killed the API --
# so an API could outlive the UI, keep the port, and answer the next run's browser with a
# verdict it cached at its own startup (2026-11-15: a restarted `just observatory-dev 8250`
# printed `bind: address already in use` while Vite proxied to the previous day's process).
#
# `--strictPort`: a Vite whose port is taken picks the next free one by itself, and the
# browser tab on the named port is then some other process's board. `--tillr-drain` names
# the planner built beside the API, so Waiting never asks whatever PATH holds.
#
# Every value below comes from the recipe (tools/observatory/run.sh), which resolves the port scheme, the store and
# the caller's passthrough flags once, so this file carries no addresses of its own.
#
# LIVE RELOAD: the API runs under `air` (pinned in `mise.toml`), configured in
# `tools/tillr-view/.air.toml`, which also holds the serve command line that used to be this
# line. air watches the Go this binary is built from (tools/tillr-view and the modules its
# go.mod replaces), waits for a burst of changes to settle, rebuilds with run.sh's own
# command, and replaces the API on the same port only when the build succeeds; a failed build
# prints its error here and the old API keeps answering. air is a child of hivemind and
# stops the API it started when it is signalled, so the one-tree guarantee above holds.
#
# TILLR_VIEW_API_ARGS (read by `.air.toml`) is the caller's passthrough as one whitespace-separated string, split
# here. Every flag `tillr-view serve` takes is `--flag value` or `--flag=value`, so a value
# carrying a space is the one case that has to be spelled `--flag=value`. The flags this
# line supplies are all defaults: the passthrough comes last, and Go's flag package lets
# the last occurrence win, which is what makes a caller's `--store` beat this one.
api: cd "$TILLR_VIEW_SRC" && exec air -c .air.toml
ui: exec npm --prefix "$TILLR_VIEW_WEB" run dev -- --config vite.config.ts --port "$TILLR_VIEW_PORT" --strictPort --host "$TILLR_VIEW_UI_HOST"
